E,L,M,C,X="Engine and Fuel","Electrical","Coatings and Paint","Maintenance","x"
TIPS=[
(E,"Why won't my outboard start? It cranks but doesn't fire.","Bad or stale fuel (older than 30 days, especially ethanol blends). The #1 cause|Clogged fuel filter or water separator|Primer bulb not pumped firm|Fouled spark plugs|Portable tank vent left closed, creating a vacuum","Squeeze the primer bulb, open the tank vent, check fuel freshness, replace the fuel filter, and pull the plugs. Replace them if black, oily, or worn."),
(L,"Why does my outboard click or do nothing when I turn the key?","Dead or weak battery (should read 12.4V or higher)|Corroded or loose battery terminals|Engine not in neutral (neutral safety switch)|Kill switch lanyard not attached|Bad starter solenoid or starter motor|Blown ignition fuse","Clean terminals, charge or test the battery, confirm neutral, reattach the kill switch clip, then check fuses. A single click usually points to the solenoid or starter."),
(E,"Why won't my outboard start after sitting all winter or for a long time?","Ethanol-damaged fuel that absorbed water or turned to varnish|Clogged carburetor jets|Battery self-discharge|Stuck components from corrosion","Drain old fuel and refill with fresh, ethanol-free gas if possible. Add stabilizer, clean or rebuild the carburetor, and charge the battery. Next time, stabilize the fuel and fog the cylinders before storage."),
(E,"Why does my outboard start and then die immediately?","Fuel starvation from a clogged filter, kinked line, or failing pump|Dirty carburetor that can't hold idle|Choke or primer issues (too lean or too rich)|Faulty kill switch or ignition part|Overheat or low-oil protection shutting it down","Inspect fuel lines for kinks or cracks, replace the filter, and check that the primer bulb stays firm while running. Confirm cooling water flow and oil level, and look for warning alarms."),
(E,"Why won't my outboard start when it's hot?","Vapor lock: fuel boils in the lines, common in South Florida heat|Flooded engine from over-priming or too much choke|Ignition coil or stator that fails when hot|Weak fuel pump under heat","Let the engine cool 10 to 15 minutes. If flooded, open the throttle fully (no choke) and crank to clear the fuel. Squeeze the primer bulb to push out vapor. If it repeats, have the ignition tested."),
(E,"Why won't my inboard engine start? It cranks but doesn't fire.","Closed fuel valve, clogged filter, or failed fuel pump|Bad or stale gas|No spark: worn plugs, bad coil, cracked distributor cap, failed ignition module|Flooded engine from over-cranking or a stuck choke or carb float|Engine kill or safety switch fault","Check the fuel valve and filter, then confirm fresh fuel. Pull a plug, ground it, and check for spark. If flooded, hold the throttle wide open and crank 10 seconds. Always run the blower 4 minutes before any restart attempt."),
(E,"Why does my outboard run rough or stall at idle?","Dirty carburetor or injectors from ethanol varnish|Fouled or wrong-gap spark plugs|Idle speed set too low or a faulty idle circuit|Air leak at a cracked intake hose or loose carb mount|Water in the fuel","Replace the plugs, add a marine fuel system cleaner, and replace the fuel filter. If it persists, the carb or injectors need professional cleaning."),
(E,"Why is my outboard losing power or not reaching full RPM?","Wrong or damaged propeller (bent blade, wrong pitch, spun hub)|Fouled hull or lower unit|Clogged filter or weak fuel pump|Overheating that triggers power-limiting mode|Overloaded boat or poor trim","Inspect the prop, check wide-open-throttle RPM against the manufacturer's range, clean the hull, and replace fuel filters. On EFI engines, check for stored fault codes."),
(E,"What do white, blue, and black smoke mean on my engine?","White: water or coolant burning, or condensation on a cold start. Persistent white can mean a blown head gasket or cracked block|Blue: oil burning. Normal on 2-strokes, but on a 4-stroke it means worn rings or valve seals|Black: running too rich from a bad choke, clogged air filter, or carb or injector problem","Blue smoke on a 4-stroke or persistent white smoke needs a mechanic. For black smoke, check the air filter, choke, and fuel delivery."),
(E,"Why is my boat engine overheating?","Worn water pump impeller (the most common cause)|Clogged water intake or cooling passages|Stuck or failed thermostat|Closed seacock or clogged raw-water strainer (inboards)|Low coolant on closed-cooling inboards","Shut the engine off immediately. Check the intake and strainer, and confirm a strong tell-tale stream. Replace the impeller every 1 to 2 years and flush the engine after every saltwater use."),
(E,"Is E15 or E10 gas safe for my boat?","E10 (10% ethanol) is approved by most manufacturers, but it attracts moisture and degrades quickly|E15 (15% ethanol) is not approved for marine engines and can void warranties and damage fuel systems|Ethanol-free recreational-grade fuel (90 to 93 octane) is best for storage and older boats","Use E10 or less, buy from high-turnover stations, add a stabilizer with ethanol treatment, and keep tanks nearly full in storage. Older fiberglass tanks and rubber lines are especially vulnerable."),
(E,"How do I know if there's water in my fuel, and what do I do?","Condensation in a partly empty tank|Contaminated fuel from a station|Leaky fuel cap or vent letting in rain or spray|Phase separation in ethanol blends left sitting","Check the clear water-separating filter bowl for a layer at the bottom. Drain it and replace the filter. If contamination is heavy, pump out the tank and refill. Never run an engine on suspect fuel."),
(E,"Why does my engine hesitate or bog when I accelerate?","Lean mixture from a clogged jet, filter, or weak pump|Fouled plugs or a weak coil|Old fuel|Throttle cable out of adjustment or sticking linkage|Failing fuel or lift pump (common on inboards)","Replace the filter and plugs, use fresh fuel, check the throttle linkage, and confirm fuel pressure on EFI engines."),
(L,"Why does my boat battery keep dying?","Parasitic drain from a bilge pump, stereo, or electronics wired directly to the battery|Old battery (typically 3 to 5 years)|Bad charging system: alternator, stator, or rectifier|Corroded connections|Left switched on or never fully charged between trips","Test resting voltage (12.6V or more is full). With the engine running you should see 13.5 to 14.7V. Clean terminals, turn the battery switch off at the dock, and use a smart charger or maintainer."),
(L,"Why is my bilge pump not working, or running constantly?","Blown fuse or tripped breaker|Stuck or fouled float switch (debris, slime, oil)|Corroded wiring or bad ground|Clogged impeller or strainer|Running constantly: a float stuck up, or a real leak","Test manually with the switch, clean the float, check the fuse, and inspect connections. If it runs constantly, look for a leak at hull fittings, shaft packing, or the transom drain."),
(L,"Why are my navigation lights not working?","Burned-out bulb or failed LED|Corroded sockets and connections (very common in saltwater)|Blown fuse or tripped breaker|Bad ground wire|Faulty switch","Check the fuse, then the bulb, then test the socket for 12V. Clean contacts with a wire brush and apply dielectric grease. Lights are legally required between sunset and sunrise, so fix this before night operation."),
(L,"Why do my GPS or fish finder keep rebooting or losing power?","Voltage drop from undersized wire or a long run|Loose or corroded connectors|Weak battery that sags under load, especially at engine start|Shared circuit with high-draw gear (trim, windlass, stereo)|Bad ground","Measure voltage at the unit during operation. Use a dedicated fused circuit, upsize the wire gauge, or add a separate house battery to isolate electronics from the starter."),
(L,"What's the difference between starting and deep-cycle batteries, and how should I wire two?","Starting (cranking) batteries deliver a big burst of power for a short time|Deep-cycle batteries deliver steady power for hours and tolerate discharge|Dual-purpose batteries do both, but neither perfectly","For most boats, use a starting battery for the engine and a separate deep-cycle for electronics, linked with a 1-2-Both-Off switch or an automatic charging relay so both charge from the engine but stay isolated when discharging."),
(L,"Why is my power trim and tilt not working?","Blown trim fuse|Low fluid in the reservoir|Bad trim relay or switch|Corroded wiring at the pump motor|Failed pump motor","Check the fuse, then the fluid level (use manufacturer-specified fluid). Listen for the pump humming when you press the switch. If it hums but doesn't move, suspect low fluid or an internal leak. Use the manual release valve to lower the engine if stuck."),
(L,"Why is my tachometer or gauge reading wrong or not working?","Loose or corroded wires behind the dash|Bad ground|Failed sender (temp, fuel, or oil pressure)|Wrong tach signal setting (pulse or cylinder count)|Failing gauge or rectifier","Inspect wiring and ground, clean connections, and verify the tach setting for your engine. Test the sender with a multimeter or swap it. Never ignore a real oil or temperature warning without confirming it's a gauge fault."),
(L,"What is galvanic corrosion and how do I prevent it?","Dissimilar metals in saltwater (such as stainless and aluminum)|Depleted sacrificial anodes (zincs)|Stray current from poor shore power wiring, on your boat or a neighbor's|Bad bonding system","Inspect anodes monthly and replace at 50% wear. Use zinc in saltwater, aluminum in brackish or mixed water, and magnesium in freshwater. Install a galvanic isolator on shore power and keep bonding wires intact."),
(M,"When should I repaint my boat's bottom?","Ablative (self-polishing) paint: every 1 to 3 years depending on product and use|Hard (modified epoxy) paint: every 1 to 2 years, can be scrubbed and reused|Trailered boats in freshwater rarely need bottom paint","Repaint when growth returns quickly after cleaning, paint is thin or chalky, the underlying color shows through, or barnacles stick easily. Warm saltwater like Florida wears paint faster and grows fouling faster."),
(M,"What's the difference between ablative and hard bottom paint?","Ablative slowly wears away to release biocide. Best for boats used regularly or kept in a slip, and recoats without much sanding|Hard paint doesn't wear and stays on the hull. Best for fast boats or boats frequently hauled and scrubbed, but needs sanding before recoat","Use ablative for slow-to-moderate cruisers and slip boats, hard paint for faster boats. Never mix types without checking compatibility, and use a barrier coat if there's any osmosis risk."),
(M,"Can I put regular bottom paint on an aluminum boat?","Most bottom paints contain copper, which causes severe galvanic corrosion on aluminum hulls, outboard lower units, and drives","Use a copper-free antifouling paint made for aluminum, or a barrier-coat system. Read labels carefully, including for outboard and sterndrive lower units, where only specific coatings are safe."),
(M,"Why is my bottom paint peeling, flaking, or bubbling?","Poor surface prep (oil, wax, or gloss not removed)|Incompatible paint layers|Moisture in the hull or osmotic blistering|Too many layers built up over the years|Painting in the wrong temperature or humidity","Strip back to a sound surface, sand, clean with the recommended solvent, apply a compatible primer or barrier coat, and follow the temperature and recoat window on the label."),
(M,"How do I remove oxidation and chalkiness from fiberglass gelcoat?","UV exposure breaking down the gelcoat surface|No regular waxing|Harsh chemicals or abrasive cleaners|Storage in direct sun without a cover","Wash with marine boat soap, use an oxidation remover, then compound and polish. Finish with marine wax or a ceramic coating. Wax twice a year (more in Florida). Heavy oxidation may need wet sanding first."),
(M,"When should I repaint my topsides instead of just polishing?","Gelcoat worn through, with the fiberglass weave showing|Deep chalking that doesn't respond to compounding|Cracks, crazing, or fading across large areas|Stains that won't come out","Gelcoat usually lasts 10 to 20 years with care, and a good two-part polyurethane topside paint lasts 5 to 10. If polishing restores the shine, there's no need to repaint."),
(M,"What causes osmotic blisters on a fiberglass hull?","Water absorbed through the gelcoat into the laminate, reacting with resin to form fluid-filled pockets|Boats kept in the water year-round with no barrier coat|Poor original lamination or moisture-prone resin","Small blisters can be ground, dried, and filled. Widespread blistering means peeling the gelcoat, drying the hull completely, and applying an epoxy barrier coat system. Get a surveyor or yard to confirm severity first."),
(M,"How do I prep a boat hull for painting?","Wash and degrease with a marine solvent|Sand to remove gloss (grit depends on the paint; check the label)|Repair cracks, gouges, and blisters with epoxy filler|Clean again and wipe with solvent right before painting|Prime with the system-recommended primer|Paint within the right temperature and humidity window","Prep is 80% of a lasting paint job. Using one manufacturer's system from primer to topcoat avoids compatibility problems."),
(C,"How often should I service my outboard engine?","Every 100 hours or once a year, whichever comes first, for most 4-strokes|Annually for most recreational use","Service includes engine oil and filter, lower unit gear oil, spark plugs, fuel filter, water pump impeller check, anodes, grease points, and a check of belt or timing components. Follow your owner's manual and keep records for warranty and resale."),
(C,"How do I winterize an outboard or inboard boat?","Stabilize the fuel and run the engine to circulate it|Change oil, filter, and lower unit gear oil|Fog the cylinders (outboards)|Flush the cooling system; on inboards, drain the block and manifolds or fill with non-toxic antifreeze|Remove and charge the battery|Clean and cover the boat so it can drain and breathe","South Florida rarely sees hard freezes, but storage prep still matters for long periods of non-use."),
(C,"Why is my boat steering stiff or hard to turn?","Dry or corroded steering cable (mechanical systems)|Low hydraulic fluid or air in the lines|Seized tilt tube or pivot points|Prop or lower unit damage|Worn or damaged helm pump","Grease all steering fittings and the tilt tube. Check hydraulic fluid and bleed the system if needed. If the cable itself is stiff, replace it. A cable seized from corrosion rarely recovers."),
(C,"Why is there milky or leaking gear oil in my lower unit?","Failed prop shaft seals, often from fishing line wrapped around the shaft|Damaged drain or vent plug gaskets|Cracked housing from impact|Corrosion around the seals or shaft","Milky oil means water intrusion, so don't ignore it. Drain, inspect for metal particles, replace the seals, and pressure test the unit. Catching it early can save the gears and bearings."),
(C,"Why isn't water coming out of my outboard's tell-tale (pee stream)?","Clogged tell-tale outlet|Worn or failed water pump impeller|Blocked water intake on the lower unit|Stuck thermostat|Cracked or leaking water tube","Shut the engine off. Clear the outlet with a thin wire or compressed air, check the intake screens, and replace the impeller if it's more than a couple of years old. Running without water flow can ruin an engine in minutes."),
(C,"Why do I have vibration in my boat at speed?","Bent or damaged propeller|Fishing line or debris wrapped around the shaft|Bent prop shaft or worn cutlass bearing (inboards)|Misaligned engine or drive coupling|Loose or worn motor mounts|Fouled hull with uneven growth","Inspect and balance or replace the prop, clear the shaft of any line, and check alignment and mounts. Vibration left unchecked destroys seals, bearings, and mounts."),
]
CALL={E:"the engine still won't run after the fixes above, you smell fuel in the bilge, or there's smoke or overheating.",L:"you see melted wire, smell burning, or the problem returns after cleaning connections. Never guess with electrical faults on a boat.",M:"the hull shows blisters, soft spots, or widespread flaking. Hull damage gets more expensive the longer it waits.",C:"you find metal in the gear oil, water in the lower unit, or you're not comfortable doing the work yourself."}
